4,4,3,0,0,0],[0,0,0,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,0,0,0]]}]},"teaching_variant":"canonical","teacher_models":["claude-opus-5-5"],"explanation":"## Solving the puzzle\n\n**What the grid contains.** Each input holds one or more \"onion\" objects. These are rectangles made of nested concentric layers, and each layer has its own colour. For example, the large object in pair 1 reads 6 → 3 → 4 → 2 → 1 going from the outside in. The small one reads 8 → 2 → 1.\n\n**What changes.** No cell moves, and the shapes stay exactly the same. Only the colours change, and they change by **reversing the order of the layers**:\n- Pair 1, large object: 6,3,4,2,1 becomes 1,2,4,3,6. The outer frame is now 1 and the core is now 6.\n- Pair 1, small object: 8,2,1 becomes 1,2,8.\n- Pair 2: 1,3,8,2 becomes 2,8,3,1.\n- Pair 3, top object: 1,6,8 becomes 8,6,1. The middle layer 6 stays put because it is the centre of an odd-length sequence.\n- Pair 3, bottom object: 2,4,1,8 becomes 8,1,4,2.\n- Pair 4: 8,7,4,3 becomes 3,4,7,8, and 2,1,3 becomes 3,1,2.\n\nThe fact sheet's colour transitions agree with this. In pair 4, for instance, the changes are 7→4, 8→3, 4→7 and 3→8 for the big object, plus 2→3 and 3→2 for the small one.\n\n**How to find the layer order in code.**\n1. Take each 4-connected component of non-background cells, ignoring colour differences. Each component is one onion.\n2. Within an onion, every colour is used for exactly one layer, and the layers are strictly nested. So the outer layer has the largest bounding box. Sorting the onion's colours by bounding-box area, largest first, gives the outside-to-inside sequence.\n3. Build a mapping that sends the colour at position i to the colour at position n−1−i, then apply it to every cell of the onion.\n\n**Test input.** The colours are distinct within each object, so the bounding-box ordering works there too:\n- Top-left object: 3,6,1,4 becomes 4,1,6,3.\n- Right object: 5,4,8 becomes 8,4,5.\n- Bottom object: 6,8,2,4,3 becomes 3,4,2,8,6.\n\n**Emitted stages.** The program emits one canvas after each onion is recoloured. The last emit shows the finished grid.","code":"def transform(grid):\n    g = np.array(grid)\n    bg = background(g)\n    out = g.copy()\n    comps = components(g != bg, diagonal=False)\n    for cells in comps:\n        cols = {}\n        for (y, x) in cells:\n            cols.setdefault(int(g[y, x]), []).append((y, x))\n        def area(c):\n            y0, x0, y1, x1 = bbox(cols[c])\n            return (y1 - y0 + 1) * (x1 - x0 + 1)\n        order = sorted(cols.keys(), key=lambda c: -area(c))\n        rev = order[::-1]\n        mapping = {order[i]: rev[i] for i in range(len(order))}\n        for (y, x) in cells:\n            out[y, x] = mapping[int(g[y, x])]\n        emit(out, f\"Reversed the layer colours of the nested object {order} -> {rev}\")\n    emit(out, \"All nested objects have their layer order reversed\")\n    return out\n","candidate_tactics":{"objects":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"Take each 4-connected component of non-background cells, ignoring colour differences."}],"selection":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"Only the colours change, and they change by **reversing the order of the layers**:"},{"source":"final explanation","text":"So the outer layer has the largest bounding box."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"Sorting the onion's colours by bounding-box area, largest first, gives the outside-to-inside sequence."}],"recolour":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"**Emitted stages.** The program emits one canvas after each onion is recoloured."}],"order":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"Sorting the onion's colours by bounding-box area, largest first, gives the outside-to-inside sequence."}],"crop":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"So the outer layer has the largest bounding box."}],"layers":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"These are rectangles made of nested concentric layers, and each layer has its own colour."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"Only the colours change, and they change by **reversing the order of the layers**:"},{"source":"final explanation","text":"The middle layer 6 stays put because it is the centre of an odd-length sequence."}]},"provenance":{"reference_assisted":false,"query_labels_used_for_rule_diagnosis":false},"artifact_sha256":"81aed8b57b8837e05d4f64cdd7c39c071ed711a9126cdadc601e4712ccb8f3f9","task_sha256":"91f8447006b67598e9fb0522835d01f1cb5a19c513b6597a41e47a2d92c03cdc","validation":"Frozen corpus recorded successful replay on teaching fixtures; synthetic generalization not established."}
